      "text": "To find the 25th term of the arithmetic sequence \\(2, 5, 8, \\ldots\\), we first need to identify the first term and the common difference of the sequence.\n\nThe first term \\(a_1\\) of the sequence is 2.\n\nThe common difference \\(d\\) is the difference between any two consecutive terms. For example, the difference between the second term and the first term is \\(5 - 2 = 3\\). Therefore, the common difference \\(d\\) is 3.\n\nThe formula for the \\(n\\)-th term of an arithmetic sequence is given by:\n\\[\na_n = a_1 + (n-1)d\n\\]\nHere, \\(a_n\\) is the \\(n\\)-th term, \\(a_1\\) is the first term, \\(d\\) is the common difference, and \\(n\\) is the term number. We need to find the 25th term (\\(a_{25}\\)).\n\nSubstituting \\(a_1 = 2\\), \\(d = 3\\), and \\(n = 25\\) into the formula, we get:\n\\[\na_{25} = 2 + (25-1) \\cdot 3\n\\]\nSimplify the expression inside the parentheses first:\n\\[\na_{25} = 2 + 24 \\cdot 3\n\\]\nNext, perform the multiplication:\n\\[\na_{25} = 2 + 72\n\\]\nFinally, add the numbers together:\n\\[\na_{25} = 74\n\\]\nThus, the value of the 25th term of the arithmetic sequence is \\(\\boxed{74}\\).",
